   "question": "How do I use gti --amend to fix my last commit message?",
   "real_tool": "git",
   "run": {
    "status": "answered",
    "final": "The `git commit --amend` command is used to modify the most recent commit. Here's how to use it to fix your commit message:\n\n## Basic Usage\n\n```bash\ngit commit --amend\n```\n\nThis will open your default text editor (usually `vi` or `nano`) and allow you to edit the commit message. When you save and close the editor, the commit will be updated with your new message.\n\n## With a New Message (No Editor)\n\nIf you want to provide the new message directly without opening an editor:\n\n```bash\ngit commit --amend -m \"Your new commit message here\"\n```\n\n## Example\n\n```bash\n# If you just want to change the message\ngit commit --amend -m \"Fixed the bug in the login module\"\n\n# Or if you want to edit it in your editor\ngit commit --amend\n```\n\n## Important Notes\n\n1. **Amending a Public Commit**: If you've already pushed your commit to a remote repository, you'll need to use `git push --force` to update it. This can be problematic if others have based work on your commit.\n\n2. **Amending a Private Commit**: If the commit hasn't been pushed yet, you can simply amend it and push normally.\n\n3. **Preserving Changes**: The `--amend` command keeps all the changes from the original commit and only modifies the commit message. If you also need to change the actual code changes, you'll need to use `git commit --amend --no-edit` followed by `git reset --soft HEAD~1` and then create a new commit.\n\n4. **Safety**: It's generally good practice to avoid amending commits that have already been pushed to shared repositories, as it can confuse collaborators.",
    "calls": [],
    "turns": [
     {
      "prompt_tokens": 307,
      "completion_tokens": 366,
      "finish_reason": "stop",
      "text": "The `git commit --amend` command is used to modify the most recent commit. Here's how to use it to fix your commit message:\n\n## Basic Usage\n\n```bash\ngit commit --amend\n```\n\nThis will open your default text editor (usually `vi` or `nano`) and allow you to edit the commit message. When you save and close the editor, the commit will be updated with your new message.\n\n## With a New Message (No Editor)\n\nIf you want to provide the new message directly without opening an editor:\n\n```bash\ngit commit --amend -m \"Your new commit message here\"\n```\n\n## Example\n\n```bash\n# If you just want to change the message\ngit commit --amend -m \"Fixed the bug in the login module\"\n\n# Or if you want to edit it in your editor\ngit commit --amend\n```\n\n## Important Notes\n\n1. **Amending a Public Commit**: If you've already pushed your commit to a remote repository, you'll need to use `git push --force` to update it. This can be problematic if others have based work on your commit.\n\n2. **Amending a Private Commit**: If the commit hasn't been pushed yet, you can simply amend it and push normally.\n\n3. **Preserving Changes**: The `--amend` command keeps all the changes from the original commit and only modifies the commit message. If you also need to change the actual code changes, you'll need to use `git commit --amend --no-edit` followed by `git reset --soft HEAD~1` and then create a new commit.\n\n4. **Safety**: It's generally good practice to avoid amending commits that have already been pushed to shared repositories, as it can confuse collaborators."
