    "final": "The `systemctl --failed` command is used to list all systemd units that have failed. Here's how to use it:\n\n## Basic Usage\n\n```bash\nsystemctl --failed\n```\n\nThis will display a list of all units that have failed, showing their status and any relevant information.\n\n## Output Format\n\nThe output typically shows:\n- **UNIT** - The unit name (e.g., `nginx.service`, `docker.service`)\n- **LOAD** - Whether the unit is loaded (loaded, not-found, error, etc.)\n- **ACTIVE** - Whether the unit is active (active, inactive, activating, deactivating)\n- **SUB** - The substate (e.g., `dead`, `running`, `exited`, `failed`)\n- **DESCRIPTION** - A human-readable description of the unit\n\n## Examples\n\n### List failed units\n```bash\nsystemctl --failed\n```\n\n### List only failed units (same as above)\n```bash\nsystemctl --failed\n```\n\n### List failed units with more details\n```bash\nsystemctl --failed --no-pager\n```\n\n### List failed units and show their status\n```bash\nsystemctl --failed --no-pager --no-legend\n```\n\n## Common Use Cases\n\n1. **Check for failed services after a reboot:**\n   ```bash\n   systemctl --failed\n   ```\n\n2. **Check if there are any failed units:**\n   ```bash\n   systemctl --failed | grep -q \"failed\" && echo \"There are failed units\" || echo \"No failed units\"\n   ```\n\n3. **Get the count of failed units:**\n   ```bash\n   systemctl --failed | wc -l\n   ```\n\n4. **List only failed services (not sockets, timers, etc.):**\n   ```bash\n   systemctl --failed | grep \".service\"\n   ```\n\n## Additional Options\n\n- `--no-pager` - Don't pipe output through a pager (less, more, etc.)\n- `--no-legend` - Don't print the legend (LOAD, ACTIVE, SUB, DESCRIPTION)\n- `--all` - Show all units, including those that are not loaded\n\n## Troubleshooting Failed Units\n\nAfter identifying failed units, you can:\n- Check the unit's status: `systemctl status <unit-name>`\n- View unit logs: `journalctl -u <unit-name>`\n- Check unit configuration: `systemctl cat <unit-name>`\n-",
